## syntax

[section adapted from][junegunn]

Sometimes fuzzy matching is not enough. fzf implements its own simple search
syntax to augment fuzzy matching.

#### matching

| Token     | Match type                              | Description                                        |
| --------- | --------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `sbtrkt`  | fuzzy-match                             | Items that include sbtrkt characters in that order |
| `'wild`   | exact-match (quoted)                    | Items that include wild                            |
| `'wild'`  | exact-boundary-match (quoted both ends) | Items that include wild at word boundaries         |
| `^music`  | prefix-exact-match                      | Items that start with music                        |
| `.mp3$`   | suffix-exact-match                      | Items that end with .mp3                           |
| `!fire`   | inverse-exact-match                     | Items that do not include fire                     |
| `!^music` | inverse-prefix-exact-match              | Items that do not start with music                 |
| `!.mp3$`  | inverse-suffix-exact-match              | Items that do not end with .mp3                    |

#### exactness

If you don’t prefer fuzzy matching and do not wish to “quote” every word, start
fzf with -e or --exact option. Note that when --exact is set, '-prefix
“unquotes” the term.

#### operators

| rune   | meaning                                                                                           | example                                                                                          |
| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `' '`  | term separator. a search for each term delimited by spaces is conducted separately                | `git add` gitadd is more likely to filter easily                                                 |
| `'\|'` | logical or. a single pipe character term acts as an OR operator. For example, the following query | `^core go$ \| rb$ \| py$` matches entries that start with core and end with either go, rb, or py |

## cli

| example                                                   | explanation                                                                  |
| --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `fzf`                                                     | To fuzzy find files in current directory                                     |
| `cat $(fzf)`                                              | To fuzzy find a file and cat it                                              |
| `fzf +i`                                                  | Case sensitive search                                                        |
| `fzf -i`                                                  | Case insensitve search                                                       |
| `rm -rf $(fzf --multi)` or `fzf --multi \| xargs -n1 rm`  | Select multiple files (TAB to select, Shift+TAB to un-select) to delete      |
| `fzf --preview "file {}"`                                 | Run a preview command with fzf, specifiy a command inside the double quotes. |
| `find . -type f -name "*.txt" \| fzf --preview "head {}"` | Find files from find command and preview it with fzf                         |
| `fzf --border sharp`                                      | Display border around fzf output                                             |
| `fd '\.txt$' --type f \| fzf --multi > output.txt`        | Output only selected files and pipe it to a file                             |

